### II. HIGH CONCURRENCY & ENTERPRISE BATCH ARCHITECTURE\
\
1. HIGH-SCALE DATASET ASSUMPTIONS (10,000+ RECORDS):\
   - All backend processing, imports, purges, calculation engines, and lookups must scale to 10,000+ records without memory exhaustion or timeouts.\
   - Never rely on single-record framework convenience APIs (e.g., standard `wp_insert_user`, `wp_delete_user`, or individual `update_user_meta` calls inside loops) during multi-record batch processing without explicit transaction batching.\
\
2. COMPUTATIONAL HOISTING & COMPLEXITY:\
   - Never execute CPU-heavy operations (Bcrypt hashing, regex compilation, sanitization maps) inside loops. Hoist all CPU-heavy work outside the iteration loop.\
   - O(N^2) nested array scanning or repeated database lookups across dataset rows are strictly prohibited. Pre-fetch datasets in bulk and index them into single-key hashtables (`array_flip`, associative key maps) for O(1) constant-time lookup.\
\
3. ATOMIC I/O BATCHING & SCALAR ISOLATION:\
   - Never hydrate full ORM objects (`WP_User`, `WP_Post`) inside processing loops. Fetch scalar primitive IDs only (`'fields' => 'ID'`).\
   - Wrap multi-row database insertions, updates, or deletions inside explicit MySQL Transactions (`$wpdb->query('START TRANSACTION')` ... `$wpdb->query('COMMIT')`).\
\
4. DRY ARCHITECTURE & SHARED HELPER EXTRACTION:\
   - Never duplicate multi-stage data pipelines, parsers, or ingestion loops across synchronous (POST) and asynchronous (AJAX) execution paths. Extract shared operations into dedicated, modular helper functions.\
\
